---

<LegalPage
  title="Privacy Policy — RAS Code"
  description="What RAS Code and RAS Connect process and store, and how to exercise your data protection rights."
  heading="RAS Code Privacy Policy"
  lede="RAS Code is open-source software that runs on your own machines and sends opt-out anonymous telemetry. RAS Connect is an optional hosted service, operated by Richard Solomou as an individual, that links your devices to your own RAS Code server and delivers mobile notifications. This policy also covers analytics, masked session replay, and error reporting in official RAS Code mobile builds."
  effectiveDate="2026-08-28"
  lastUpdated="2026-08-31"
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="introduction">
    <p class="section-number">01</p>
    <h2>Introduction</h2>
    <p>
      This Privacy Policy explains how Richard Solomou (“I”, “me”) collects, uses, and shares
      information in connection with RAS Code, RAS Connect, and the RAS Code website. RAS Connect is
      run by one person, not a company.
    </p>
    <p>
      RAS Code itself is MIT-licensed software that you download and run. Its server sends anonymous
      product and reliability telemetry to PostHog by default, whether or not you sign in to RAS
      Connect. You can disable server, web, and desktop telemetry with
      <code>RAS_CODE_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=false</code>. Official mobile builds can send the limited
      analytics, masked replay, and error information described below whether or not you sign in.
      Source builds without the PostHog mobile configuration do not send mobile telemetry. Signing
      in to RAS Connect is optional.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="scope">
    <p class="section-number">02</p>
    <h2>What This Policy Covers</h2>
    <p>
      This policy covers RAS Connect: the hosted relay that links RAS Code environments to your
      account, issues short-lived connection credentials, provisions managed network endpoints, and
      delivers push notifications and Live Activities to the RAS Code mobile app. It also covers the
      RAS Code marketing website, anonymous telemetry sent by the RAS Code server and its
      authenticated web and desktop clients, and analytics, session replay, and error reporting in
      official mobile builds.
    </p>
    <p>
      This policy does not cover the coding agents, model providers, repositories, or operating
      systems you connect RAS Code to. Those are chosen and configured by you, and they process
      information under their own terms and privacy policies. It also does not cover a RAS Code
      server running on your own machine, which you operate yourself, except for the anonymous
      telemetry described below.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="information">
    <p class="section-number">03</p>
    <h2>Information I Process</h2>

    <h3>Account Information</h3>
    <p>
      Authentication is provided by Clerk. When you sign in, RAS Connect receives an account
      identifier for you from Clerk and uses it to scope everything below. The profile information
      you give to Clerk, such as your email address, name, and sign-in method, is held by Clerk under
      its own privacy policy.
    </p>

    <h3>Environment Links</h3>
    <p>
      When you link a RAS Code environment to your account, RAS Connect stores the environment
      identifier, the label you chose for it, the environment's public key, its HTTP and WebSocket
      endpoint addresses, the kind of endpoint in use, your notification and Live Activity settings
      for it, the identifier of the device that created the link, and timestamps.
    </p>

    <h3>Connection Credentials</h3>
    <p>
      Credentials that let a client connect to your environment are short-lived and are bound to the
      requesting client's key. RAS Connect stores a hash of each credential, together with the
      environment identifier and public key, so a credential can be checked and revoked. The
      credential itself is not stored. Short-lived proof identifiers are recorded so that a proof
      cannot be replayed.
    </p>

    <h3>Managed Endpoints</h3>
    <p>
      If you enable a managed endpoint, RAS Connect derives a hostname for your environment and
      routes traffic through a shared Cloudflare Worker and a per-environment Durable Object. The
      environment link and connection credential described above are the persisted routing state.
    </p>

    <h3>Mobile Devices and Push Tokens</h3>
    <p>
      If you register the mobile app for notifications, RAS Connect stores a device identifier, the
      device label you chose, the platform and major operating-system version, the app version and
      bundle identifier, the Apple Push Notification service environment, your Apple push tokens
      (including push-to-start and Live Activity tokens), and your notification preferences.
    </p>

    <h3>Agent Activity</h3>
    <p>
      When notifications or Live Activities are enabled for an environment, that environment
      publishes a short status record per thread so a notification can be composed. Each record
      contains the environment and thread identifiers, the project title, the thread title, the model
      name, a phase such as running or waiting for approval, a one-line headline and optional detail,
      an in-app deep link, and a timestamp. Titles and status text are truncated to short fixed
      lengths before storage and delivery.
    </p>

    <h3>Delivery Records</h3>
    <p>
      For each notification RAS Connect attempts to send, it records the time, your account
      identifier, the environment, thread, and device involved, the kind of notification, a short
      trailing fragment of the push token, and the response Apple returned, including any error.
      These records exist so that failed deliveries can be diagnosed and retries are not duplicated.
    </p>

    <h3>Operational Traces</h3>
    <p>
      RAS Connect emits request traces to PostHog. A trace records the request method, path, route,
      response status, duration, and your account identifier. Authorization credentials are not
      recorded in traces.
    </p>

    <h3>Anonymous RAS Code Telemetry</h3>
    <p>
      The RAS Code server sends anonymous product events, provider turn timing and token totals,
      low-cardinality metrics, a small allowlist of operational logs, and redacted server exceptions
      to PostHog. It uses a hashed coding-provider account identifier when one is available, or an
      installation-scoped anonymous identifier. It does not create a PostHog person profile.
    </p>
    <p>
      After authentication, the web and desktop clients send normalized pageviews, structural
      interaction events, Web Vitals, pointer coordinates for heatmaps, redacted browser
      exceptions, and layout-only session replays. Replay text, inputs, and element attributes are
      masked. Network bodies, network headers, console logs, referrers, query strings, fragments,
      JSON-LD, and fonts are not recorded. The mobile client does not load the browser telemetry
      SDK.
    </p>

    <h3>Mobile App Analytics, Replay, and Errors</h3>
    <p>
      Official mobile builds send stable screen names, app and operating-system versions, device type
      and model, locale, timezone, and an app-scoped identifier to PostHog. If you sign in to RAS
      Connect, the app identifies the PostHog record with your Clerk account identifier. It resets
      that identity when you sign out.
    </p>
    <p>
      Session replay records a visual representation of navigation and interactions. Text, images,
      and system views are masked before upload. Console logs and network telemetry are disabled.
      Error reporting sends uncaught JavaScript exception messages, stack traces, and unhandled
      promise rejections. Release identifiers are attached so a report can be matched to the mobile
      build or update that caused it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="not-collected">
    <p class="section-number">04</p>
    <h2>What I Do Not Store</h2>
    <p>
      When you use a managed endpoint, the relay temporarily processes the API and WebSocket payloads
      needed to carry traffic between your client and server. It does not persist normal traffic
      payloads, including:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your prompts, or the responses your coding agent returns;</li>
      <li>Your source code, files, attachments, diffs, patches, or commits;</li>
      <li>Command output, terminal contents, or thread history;</li>
      <li>Your provider or model API keys, or your repository credentials; or</li>
      <li>Other contents of the environment your RAS Code server runs on.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The short agent activity record described above is persisted only when you enable
      notifications. Nothing here is used to train artificial-intelligence models.
    </p>
    <p>
      Anonymous RAS Code telemetry does not contain prompts, agent responses, repository contents,
      terminal output, raw URLs, IP address properties, secrets, provider error text, abort reasons,
      local variables, source context, or absolute source paths.
    </p>
    <p>
      Mobile analytics does not include console logs, request URLs, network metadata, touch
      autocapture events, prompts, source code, file contents, or uploaded images. Session replay
      masks text and images, and does not include console logs or network telemetry.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="traffic">
    <p class="section-number">05</p>
    <h2>How Your Traffic Reaches Your Server</h2>
    <p>
      Local-network connections and tunnels you operate yourself bypass RAS Connect. A managed
      endpoint sends API and WebSocket traffic through the shared RAS Connect relay to the outbound
      connector running with your RAS Code server.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cloudflare terminates TLS at its edge for managed endpoints, so traffic is not end-to-end
      encrypted between your client and server and is technically visible to Cloudflare in transit.
      RAS Connect does not persist the payloads. Choose a local network or self-operated connection
      if that matters for your work.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="site">
    <p class="section-number">06</p>
    <h2>The RAS Code Website</h2>
    <p>
      The website is a static site hosted by Cloudflare. Cloudflare logs standard request
      information, including IP addresses, as part of serving and protecting it. The site loads web fonts from
      Google Fonts, which means your browser sends a request, and therefore your IP address, to
      Google when a page loads. The site sets no analytics, advertising, or tracking cookies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="uses">
    <p class="section-number">07</p>
    <h2>How the Information Is Used</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>To authenticate you and scope your environments, devices, and credentials to your account;</li>
      <li>To link environments and issue short-lived credentials so your clients can connect;</li>
      <li>To provision and tear down managed endpoints you request;</li>
      <li>To compose and deliver the notifications and Live Activities you enable;</li>
      <li>To diagnose failures, keep the service reliable, and investigate abuse or security issues;</li>
      <li>To understand which RAS Code clients and provider features are used and improve them;</li>
      <li>To respond to you when you get in touch; and</li>
      <li>To comply with legal obligations.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Under GDPR and UK GDPR terms, the legal bases are performance of a contract for operating the
      features you enable, legitimate interests for product improvement, reliability, abuse
      prevention, and security, and legal obligation where the law requires it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="sharing">
    <p class="section-number">08</p>
    <h2>Service Providers and Disclosures</h2>
    <p>RAS Code and RAS Connect use the following providers, and no others:</p>
    <ul class="provider-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Clerk</strong> <em>(Clerk, Inc.)</em>.<br />
        Account authentication and session management.<br />
        <a href="https://clerk.com/legal/privacy">https://clerk.com/legal/privacy</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cloudflare</strong> <em>(Cloudflare, Inc.)</em>.<br />
        Hosts the relay Worker, Durable Objects, the RAS Code website, and the relay's queues, and
        provides DNS for managed endpoints and the tunnel used to reach the database.<br />
        <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/policies/privacy/"
          >https://www.cloudflare.com/policies/privacy/</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Apple</strong> <em>(Apple Inc.)</em>.<br />
        The Apple Push Notification service delivers notifications and Live Activity updates to iOS
        devices, and the App Store distributes the mobile app.<br />
        <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/">https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>PostHog</strong> <em>(PostHog, Inc.)</em>.<br />
        Stores the operational request traces, anonymous RAS Code telemetry, mobile analytics,
        masked session replays, and error reports described above.<br />
        <a href="https://posthog.com/privacy">https://posthog.com/privacy</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Expo</strong> <em>(650 Industries, Inc.)</em>.<br />
        Builds the mobile app and serves its over-the-air updates, which means the app checks for
        updates against Expo's servers.<br />
        <a href="https://expo.dev/privacy">https://expo.dev/privacy</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Google</strong> <em>(Google LLC)</em>.<br />
        Serves the website's web fonts, and distributes the mobile app through Google Play.<br />
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">https://policies.google.com/privacy</a>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Your personal information is not sold, rented, or shared for advertising. It may be disclosed
      where the law requires it, or where disclosure is necessary to investigate abuse or protect the
      rights and safety of users of the service. There is no parent company, affiliate group, or
      advertising network involved.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="retention">
    <p class="section-number">09</p>
    <h2>Retention and Deletion</h2>
    <p>
      Environment links, credential hashes, managed endpoint allocations, and device registrations
      are kept while they are active. Unlinking an environment or removing a device deletes the
      corresponding records, and unlinking also tears down the managed endpoint that belonged to it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Agent activity records are removed when the thread reaches a finished state, and a scheduled
      job removes stale ones: after two hours for a running thread, and after twenty-four hours for
      one waiting on you. Replay-protection records are removed once they expire. Operational traces
      are retained by PostHog for 14 days and then deleted. Notification delivery records are retained
      for diagnostics and are not currently deleted on a schedule; they can be deleted on request.
    </p>
    <p>
      Anonymous RAS Code telemetry follows the retention configured for the PostHog project. You can
      stop future collection by disabling telemetry before starting the server.
    </p>
    <p>
      Mobile analytics events, session replays, and error reports follow the retention settings of
      the PostHog project and can be deleted on request.
    </p>
    <p>
      To delete your RAS Connect account and everything associated with it, email
      hi@ras.sh. Deleting your RAS Connect data does not
      affect data on your own machines, in your repositories, or held by the agent providers you
      chose; delete that at the source.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="rights">
    <p class="section-number">10</p>
    <h2>Your Data Protection Rights</h2>
    <p>
      Depending on where you live, you may have the right to request access to the personal
      information held about you, to have it corrected, to have it deleted, to receive a portable
      copy, to restrict or object to how it is processed, and to withdraw consent where processing
      relies on consent. These rights are honoured for everyone who asks, regardless of location.
    </p>
    <p>
      To exercise a right, email hi@ras.sh from the address
      associated with your account. Requests are answered within one month. Verification may be
      needed before acting on a request.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you are in the EEA or the UK and are not satisfied with the response, you may complain to
      your local supervisory authority, or to the Office of the Commissioner for
      Personal Data Protection in Cyprus, where the service is operated.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="security">
    <p class="section-number">11</p>
    <h2>Security</h2>
    <p>
      Traffic to RAS Connect is encrypted in transit. Connection credentials are short-lived, bound
      to the requesting client's key, and stored only as hashes. Authorization credentials are kept
      out of traces, and delivery records store only a short trailing fragment of a push token rather
      than the token. Access to production systems is limited to the operator. Further detail is in
      the <a href="/security-policy">Security Policy</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      No system is completely secure, and no guarantee of absolute security is offered. You remain
      responsible for the machines, networks, repositories, and provider accounts you connect to RAS
      Code.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="international">
    <p class="section-number">12</p>
    <h2>International Transfers</h2>
    <p>
      RAS Connect runs on globally distributed infrastructure, so your information may be processed
      in countries other than your own, including the United States. Where a transfer out of the EEA
      or the UK requires a safeguard, it relies on the mechanisms offered by the providers named
      above, such as standard contractual clauses in their data processing terms. The service is
      operated from Cyprus.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="children">
    <p class="section-number">13</p>
    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
      RAS Connect is not intended for children under 13, and personal information is not knowingly
      collected from them. If you believe a child under 13 has provided personal information, get in
      touch and it will be deleted.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="updates">
    <p class="section-number">14</p>
    <h2>Updates to This Policy</h2>
    <p>
      This policy will change as the service changes. Revisions are posted at this URL with updated
      dates above. Material changes will be announced in the app or on the website before they take
      effect, where that is practical.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="contact">
    <p class="section-number">15</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      RAS Connect is operated by Richard Solomou as an individual. For privacy questions, access
      requests, or deletion requests, email hi@ras.sh.
    </p>
  </section>
</LegalPage>
